---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';

const flags = [
  ['--level info|warning|error', 'Choose the minimum finding level. The default is warning.'],
  ['--rulepack ai-style|reader-first', 'Choose a writing policy. Repeat the flag to run both rulepacks.'],
  ['--quiet', 'Show errors only. Equivalent to --level error.'],
  ['--format text|compact|json|json-lines|github', 'Choose readable text, dense agent feedback, machine-readable data, or GitHub annotations.'],
  ['--feedback <format>', 'Alias for --format. Use --feedback compact when an agent needs every rule group without source locations.'],
  ['--ext .md,.txt,.ts', 'Limit the file extensions SlopSift reads.'],
  ['--ignore-pattern <glob>', 'Add an ignore pattern. Repeat the flag for more than one.'],
  ['--no-ignore', 'Do not read patterns from .gitignore.'],
  ['--max-warnings <n>', 'Exit 1 when the warning count is above this number.'],
  ['--exit-zero', 'Report findings without failing the command. Runtime failures still exit 2.'],
  ['--no-error-on-unmatched-pattern', 'Allow an optional glob to match no supported files.'],
  ['--model <directory>', 'Use an explicit SlopSift ONNX model bundle.'],
  ['--no-download', 'Fail instead of using the emergency model download fallback.'],
];
const quickStart = `bunx slopsift .
bunx slopsift "docs/**/*.md" "src/**/*.{ts,tsx}"
npx slopsift . --level info`;
const agentCommand = `npx --yes slopsift@latest hook stop --rulepack ai-style --rulepack reader-first --feedback compact`;
const apiExample = `import { createSlopSift } from 'slopsift';

const slopsift = await createSlopSift();
const result = await slopsift.lintSource('draft.md', text, {
  rulepacks: ['ai-style', 'reader-first'],
  level: 'warning',
});`;
const ciExamples = `# High-confidence findings only
bunx slopsift . --quiet

# Fail when any warning appears
bunx slopsift . --max-warnings 0

# Machine-readable report without failing on prose
bunx slopsift . --format json --exit-zero`;
const skillInstall = `bunx skills add NikhilVerma/slopsift`;
const marketplaceUrl = 'https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=NikhilVerma01.slopsift-vscode';
---

<SiteLayout title="SlopSift CLI docs | Install, configure, and run locally" description="Install SlopSift with Bun or Node, lint prose and code comments, understand finding levels, configure CI, and learn how its custom NLP parser works.">
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="wordmark" href="/" aria-label="SlopSift home"><span>slop</span><span>sift</span><i aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
    <nav aria-label="Documentation navigation"><a href="/">Home</a><a href="/rules/">Rules</a><a href="https://github.com/NikhilVerma/writinglint">Source</a><a class="nav-install" href="#quick-start">Start here</a></nav>
  </header>

  <main class="docs-page">
    <header class="docs-hero">
      <p class="kicker">SlopSift documentation</p>
      <h1>The short<br />manual.</h1>
      <p>Enough to run SlopSift locally, understand what it reports, and wire it into CI. No account, API key, Python environment, or remote inference service required.</p>
    </header>

    <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <span>On this page</span>
      <a href="#quick-start">Quick start</a>
      <a href="#browser-editor">Browser editor</a>
      <a href="#vscode">VS Code</a>
      <a href="#inputs">What it reads</a>
      <a href="#findings">Findings and output</a>
      <a href="#flags">CLI flags</a>
      <a href="#ci">Using it in CI</a>
      <a href="#api">In-process API</a>
      <a href="#stop-hook">Agent stop hook</a>
      <a href="#agent-skill">Agent skill</a>
      <a href="#model">The parser model</a>
    </nav>

    <div class="docs-content">
      <section id="quick-start">
        <p class="docs-index">01 / Quick start</p>
        <h2>Run it where the writing lives.</h2>
        <p>The package and executable share the same name. Use Bun or npm without installing a separate binary.</p>
        <pre><code>{quickStart}</code></pre>
        <p>The npm package includes the compact parser weights. After npm installs the package, SlopSift works offline and does not download a second runtime.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="browser-editor">
        <p class="docs-index">Browser editor</p>
        <h2>Choose the rules before you edit.</h2>
        <p>The homepage demo and the full editor include a Rules dropdown. Choose AI style, reader-first, or both. The selected rules and parser run locally in the browser.</p>
        <p>Reader-first borrows general simplified-technical-writing techniques: introduce terms, expose relationships, keep the main point visible, and remove ornament that does not help the reader. It does not ship an external controlled dictionary or claim compliance with an external standard.</p>
        <a class="docs-source-link" href="/editor/">Open the local browser editor →</a>
      </section>

      <section id="vscode">
        <p class="docs-index">VS Code</p>
        <h2>Lint the draft while you write.</h2>
        <p>The free SlopSift extension reports local diagnostics in VS Code's Problems panel. It supports Markdown, plain text, and prose inside source-code comments. The parser weights ship inside each platform package, so normal linting does not send the document to a server.</p>
        <a class="docs-source-link" href={marketplaceUrl}>Install SlopSift from Visual Studio Marketplace →</a>
        <aside class="docs-note"><strong>Preview release:</strong> Automated extension-host tests cover Markdown and source comments. Manual testing across every supported operating system and remote workspace is still in progress.</aside>
      </section>

      <section id="inputs">
        <p class="docs-index">02 / Inputs</p>
        <h2>Prose, pages, and code comments.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Markdown and text:</strong> SlopSift reads the prose and skips frontmatter, code, link destinations, image metadata, and block quotations.</li>
          <li><strong>HTML:</strong> SlopSift reads rendered text and skips scripts, styles, templates, SVG, metadata, and code blocks.</li>
          <li><strong>Source files:</strong> SlopSift extracts line and block comments, then maps each finding back to its original file location.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignored paths:</strong> SlopSift skips dependencies, generated directories, <code>.git</code>, and anything covered by <code>.gitignore</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="findings">
        <p class="docs-index">03 / Findings</p>
        <h2>Findings need a human.</h2>
        <div class="docs-levels">
          <article><span>error</span><p>A high-confidence, specific signature. Errors fail the command.</p></article>
          <article><span>warning</span><p>Likely slop that still needs editorial judgment. Shown by default.</p></article>
          <article><span>info</span><p>A low-confidence signal worth reviewing. Use <code>--level info</code> to include it.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p>Stylish output is for people. JSON follows ESLint’s numeric severity shape while retaining SlopSift’s level, confidence, exact range, rule URL, word count, and findings per thousand words.</p>
        <p><a class="docs-source-link" href="/schemas/slopsift-result-v1.schema.json">Read the versioned JSON output schema →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section id="flags">
        <p class="docs-index">04 / CLI flags</p>
        <h2>The useful switches.</h2>
        <div class="docs-table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{flags.map(([flag, meaning]) => <tr><td><code>{flag}</code></td><td>{meaning}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Run <code>slopsift --help</code> for the canonical list shipped with your installed version.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="api">
        <p class="docs-index">API</p>
        <h2>Use the same engine inside your tool.</h2>
        <pre><code>{apiExample}</code></pre>
        <p>Reuse one instance so the local parser stays loaded. Each finding includes an exact source range.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="stop-hook">
        <p class="docs-index">Stop hook</p>
        <h2>Make the agent fix its answer before it stops.</h2>
        <pre><code>{agentCommand}</code></pre>
        <p>Put this command in a Claude Code or Codex Stop hook. It reads the hook event from standard input and writes one JSON decision. Compact feedback groups duplicate findings, omits response locations already present in the model context, and shows up to 100 findings by default.</p>
        <p>An agent can use this page as its setup source. Ask it to open <code>https://slopsift.dev/docs/</code>, install the user-level Stop hook for its host, use both rulepacks, and run a live reject-then-accept test.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="ci">
        <p class="docs-index">05 / CI</p>
        <h2>Decide how strict the gate should be.</h2>
        <pre><code>{ciExamples}</code></pre>
        <div class="docs-exits"><span><b>0</b> clean</span><span><b>1</b> lint threshold failed</span><span><b>2</b> arguments, configuration, model, or runtime failed</span></div>
        <a class="docs-source-link" href="/docs/github-actions/">Copy complete GitHub Actions workflows →</a>
      </section>

      <section id="agent-skill">
        <p class="docs-index">06 / Agent skill</p>
        <h2>Give your agent an editor.</h2>
        <p>Add the SlopSift skill with the open skills CLI. It works with Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, OpenCode, and other agents that support the Agent Skills format.</p>
        <pre><code>{skillInstall}</code></pre>
        <p>The skill tells an agent to use the project's existing Bun or Node environment, run the real SlopSift CLI, inspect exact ranges, and prioritize errors over weaker signals. It does not switch runtimes just to run the linter. It also preserves the writer's voice, leaves legitimate findings alone, and rejects authorship claims based on a lint result.</p>
        <a class="docs-source-link" href="/rules/">Browse the rules agents receive in structured output →</a>
        <aside class="docs-note"><strong>Editor integrations:</strong> The VS Code extension is available in preview. The Chrome extension remains experimental and is not published.</aside>
      </section>

      <section id="model">
        <p class="docs-index">07 / Model</p>
        <h2>Yes, it is a model we trained.</h2>
        <p>More precisely: SlopSift starts from a compact pretrained BERT-Mini English encoder and trains it for Universal Dependencies outputs: word-level parts of speech, syntactic heads, and dependency relations. Training combines UD English EWT supervision, structured distillation from a larger DeBERTa parser, and 50 controlled examples targeting grammatical relationships used by the linter.</p>
        <p>We excluded separate lexicalized template families from training and reserved them for evaluation. That reduces template leakage and tests transfer to unseen variants; it does not prove broad generalization or rule out every lexical shortcut.</p>
        <p>We quantize the selected checkpoint into an approximately 16 MiB ONNX bundle. The same weights run through ONNX Runtime Node in the CLI and ONNX Runtime Web/WASM in the browser. We used Stanza offline to label controlled examples and as an independent reference during experimentation. The runtime neither ships nor calls Stanza.</p>
        <aside class="docs-note"><strong>What it is not:</strong> SlopSift is not a classifier that guesses whether AI wrote a document. The parser builds a dependency graph; deterministic, named rules report the constructions they find.</aside>
        <a class="docs-source-link" href="https://github.com/NikhilVerma/writinglint/blob/main/packages/slopsift/MODEL.md">Read the full model card and reproducibility notes →</a>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</SiteLayout>
